From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 09:05:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 12/14] Ask for login input in a chat that goes by an id no
 user has

Without request dialogs, the login code, the 2FA password and the display
name are asked for in a conversation, whose reply is taken as the value.
That conversation went by the name "Telegram", and only a reply to that
name was taken. A front-end may hand the name back in another case:
webOS's IM transport lowercases who a message came from, so the Messaging
app replied to "telegram", the reply went out as an ordinary message,
failed with "User not found", and the login waited for a value that never
came.

The conversation now goes by "id0", the buddy name user id 0 would have,
which Telegram gives no one, and a reply is taken only when sent to it.
Being an id, it reaches the plug-in as it was given, in whichever case a
front-end keeps names, and no one's display name can be taken for it.
Being no one's, the chat holds the login alone: a front-end can clear it
of what was typed into it, the password among them, and lose nothing
else. Telegram's own service account, 777000, which sends the code, was
considered and left alone for that reason.

So that a front-end shows the prompt as from "Telegram" rather than from
"id0", the buddy is added with that alias while logging in, and taken out
of the buddy list once logged in.

The mock's purple_find_buddy found nothing while the account was not
connected. libpurple's does (blist.c, 2.14.14), and the buddy is looked
for while connecting, so the mock now does likewise.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Submitted [https://github.com/UnitedMarsupials/tdlib-purple/pull/47]
